    "rawReply": "cat <<EOL >> log.txt audit-ok\nEOL\n\nThe above one-liner uses a here-document (`<<`) with an end-of-line marker `\\n` followed by appending it directly into \"log.txt\". If you want just the shell command without any additional commands, here's what you'd use:\n\n```bash\necho 'audit-ok' >> log.txt || echo -e '\\naudit-ok\\n' > log.txt\n```\n\nThe first line attempts to append; if it's not there (which in this case shouldn't happen), it will fall back to creating the file and writing into it. The `||` operator ensures that even though we don't typically need a second command, we're prepared for any edge cases where appending fails due to permissions or some other issue.\n\nNote: In real scenarios with more complex requirements (like ensuring atomicity in case of concurrent writes), you might want something like this:\n\n```bash\necho 'audit-ok' | cat - log.txt > temp && mv temp log.txt || echo \"audit-ok\" >> log.txt\n```\n\nThis command uses a temporary file to safely append the line, minimizing race conditions."
